The Type Hierarchy & Object Model
Classified by Python's own numbers/collections.abc, not by editorial
grouping
Every element in the chemistry Periodic Table earned its place through an external, checkable authority
— IUPAC, not the book's author. This plate holds itself to the same standard: every type below is
grouped by Python's actual abstract base class hierarchy, verifiable in the standard library itself.
The Numeric Tower — numbers module
A strict inheritance ladder, each rung a real ABC, checkable via isinstance().
Complex Base of the numeric tower — complex
Real Subtype of Complex — has ordering — float
Rational Subtype of Real — expressible as a fraction — Fraction
Integral Subtype of Rational — whole numbers only — int, bool
collections.abc — the container families
Each family defined by which dunder methods a type actually implements — not by vibes.
FAMILY REQUIRES MEMBERS
Iterable __iter__ list, tuple, str, dict, set, generators
Sequence __getitem__, __len__, ordered list, tuple, str, range
Mapping __getitem__ by key, keys() dict, MappingProxyType
Set unordered, unique, supports & | - set, frozenset
Callable __call__ functions, classes, lambdas, objects with __call__
Where chemistry's Periodic Table sorts by shell-filling family (Reactive Metals, Halogens, Noble Gases), Python's real sort is
by protocol family — which dunder methods a type actually implements. bool is Integral (it IS an int subtype); range is a
Sequence but deliberately not a full list — same spirit as a noble gas: complete, stable, resistant to the usual mutation
reactions.
