Power of 10 — The Electronegativity Equivalent
Holzmann, NASA/JPL Laboratory for Reliable Software, 2006
Electronegativity explained WHY a bond resolves the way it does — not just that it does. Power of 10
does the same job for code: it is the derivation underneath this book's own Strong-Acid-vs-Weak-Acid
claim, and the rule-set that bounds Pillar V's chaos tree so every branch terminates somewhere known.
01 Restrict all control flow to simple constructs
No goto, no setjmp/longjmp, no direct or indirect recursion.
02 Bound every loop
Every loop must have a fixed, provable upper iteration bound.
03 No dynamic memory allocation after initialization
Fixed, predictable memory usage — nothing allocated mid-run.
04 No function longer than one printed page
One function, one clear purpose, reviewable at a glance.
05 Minimum two assertions per function
Assumptions stated explicitly, checked at runtime.
06 Declare data at the smallest possible scope
Narrow scope limits how much state a bug can touch.
07 Check every return value; check every parameter
No silent failure paths — this is the rule the chaos tree's 'known-good-or-logged-error' leaf rule is modeled
on directly.
08 Limit preprocessor use
Keep the code that runs close to the code that's read.
09 Restrict pointer use
No more than one level of dereferencing; no function pointers.
10 Compile with all warnings enabled, zero warnings tolerated
Every warning is treated as an error before it ships.
