HOW THIS EXPLAINS STRONG ACID VS WEAK ACID (SECTION 6, THIS BOOK)
Strong Acid: A script that bounds its loops, checks every return value, and has no unbounded
recursion runs deterministically to completion for any valid input — this book's own definition of
Strong Acid, now with a checkable rule-set behind it instead of just a label.
Weak Acid: A script with unbounded recursion, unchecked returns, or open-ended loops may
branch, early-exit, or hang depending on input — Weak Acid, and Power of 10's violations are
precisely where to look for why.
ΔEN gave the chemistry book a number: 0.96, 2.23, threshold at 1.7. Power of 10 gives this book a checklist instead of a
number — chemistry's currency is a continuous scale; code's currency here is closer to a compliance boundary, crossed or not
crossed, rule by rule. A different shape, the same job: explain why the earlier claim holds, not just assert that it does.
