Acid, Base, Salt, Neutral/Amphoteric, Complex
Deployment role, not reaction — same five buckets, stress-tested for Python
Not what a substance IS, but what ROLE it plays in its environment. Chemistry's five buckets sort
compounds by proton-donor/acceptor character. Python's sort by whether an object acts, is acted upon,
sits inert, does both symmetrically, or contains all of the above at once inside one larger structure.
Acid Acts on other files/systems, itself runs to completion (or exits)
A build pipeline; a CSV→HTML generator script
Base Holds data, waits to be acted upon, never initiates
A config file; a template; a data class with no mutating methods
Salt Settled output — stable, done, no longer needs what made it
A rendered student report page; a compiled artifact
Neutral / AmphotericNeutral: inert to the acid-base question entirely, by design. Amphoteric:
genuinely both roles, symmetrically, resolved only by who acts first —
not by context, not by type.
Neutral: math.sqrt(), a pure constant.
Amphoteric: two instances of the same class acting on each other with reversible roles
— a.transfer_to(b) vs b.transfer_to(a). This mirrors water's own mechanism (2 H2O ⇌
H3O+ + OH-) more precisely than a context-dependent validator would: identical
objects, either can play either role, decided only by who calls whom.
Complex A whole object with mixed internal roles — no single label fits the
object as a whole, only its individual parts
A model class mixing pure methods (Base-like), mutating methods (Acid-like),
and held state (Salt-like) all in one object — same spirit as an alloy or polymer
being too large/composite for a single acid-base answer.
