The State Tree
Python-only — branching execution, bounded by Power of 10
Every chemistry plate in this book's companion volume describes a single interaction, fully specified in
advance — you know K displaces Na before the reaction runs. Python's real concern once state and
repeated calls enter the picture is different in kind: the SAME code, called many times, on objects whose
internal state keeps changing between calls, can produce a genuinely different outcome depending on
history — something no chemistry table has to represent, because K's reactivity never depends on what K
did five minutes ago.
PHILOSOPHY
Modeled on an error-first approach to software: keep errors at zero by default, so that creative risktaking
becomes a deliberate choice rather than an accident — and when that risk is taken, every
path it opens is logged, not silently swallowed. This is the same discipline behind NASA/JPL's Power
of 10 (Pillar II): the branching is real and allowed, but it is bounded, and no leaf is permitted to be
an unknown state.
THE TREE RULE
Every leaf in the tree is one of exactly two things: a known-good terminal state, or an explicitly
caught and logged error. There is no third option. A silent failure, an uncaught exception that
crashes the process, or a mutation nobody logged — these are not valid leaves. They are what this
plate exists to make visible and design against.
